-- title:
--   Injectivity of substitution when κ[[X]]/(φ) is finite
-- statement:
--   Let $\kappa$ be a field and let $\varphi$ be an element of `Series κ`, that is, a pair $\varphi = (\varphi_0,\varphi_1)$ of formal power series in the two variables indexed by `Fin 2` over $\kappa$. Assume first that each component has vanishing constant coefficient, $\mathrm{constantCoeff}(\varphi_i)=0$ for both $i$ (this is what makes $\varphi$ admissible for substitution), and secondly that the quotient `FormalODModule.KerAlgebra φ`, namely the ring $\kappa[[X_0,X_1]]$ modulo the ideal generated by the set $\{\varphi_0,\varphi_1\}$, is a finite $\kappa$-module, i.e. finite-dimensional as a $\kappa$-vector space. The conclusion is that the substitution map $f \mapsto f(\varphi_0,\varphi_1)$ on $\kappa[[X_0,X_1]]$ is injective: for all power series $f$ and $g$ in two variables over $\kappa$, the equality $\mathrm{subst}\,\varphi\,f = \mathrm{subst}\,\varphi\,g$ forces $f = g$. Injectivity is stated in this two-element form rather than as a statement about the kernel of a ring homomorphism.
--
--   This is the analytic independence of the pair $\varphi$: finiteness of $\kappa[[X_0,X_1]]/(\varphi_0,\varphi_1)$ says that $\varphi_0,\varphi_1$ is a system of parameters of the two-dimensional regular local ring $\kappa[[X_0,X_1]]$, and such a system is analytically independent, so that substitution along $\varphi$ is an injective endomorphism. It is used in the study of formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-modules, where it feeds the comparison of kernel ideals under composition ([`CerednikDrinfeld.FormalODModule.exists_X_pow_mem_span_of_X_pow_mem_span_comp`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.FormalODModule.exists_X_pow_mem_span_of_X_pow_mem_span_comp)) and the injectivity of substitution for series with a kernel of prescribed degree ([`CerednikDrinfeld.FormalODModule.subst_injective_of_hasKernelOfDegree`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.FormalODModule.subst_injective_of_hasKernelOfDegree)).
